# 64-bit printf on newlib-nano

nRF52 Arduino builds link `--specs=nano.specs`. That libc is built with `_WANT_IO_LONG_LONG` undefined, so `printf` / `snprintf` / `vsnprintf` have no 64-bit conversions. `%llu`, `%lld`, and `%llx` compile cleanly and then emit only the low 32 bits. No warning, no error.

This truncated LoBBS user uuids in `LoScalar::setUint64` after the LoScalar migration. Login wrote a short id into the session row, the next command missed the user file, and the node looked logged out.

## Do

- Format a `uint64_t` by hand (digit loop), or print it as two `uint32_t` values with `LODB_UUID_FMT` / `LODB_UUID_ARGS`.
- Parse with `strtoull`. That is implemented and is fine.
- Keep `LoScalar::setUint64` on the hand formatter. `setUint32` goes through it.

## Do not

- `%llu`, `%lld`, `%llx`, `PRIu64`, `PRIx64` in any LoBBS or LoDb string that runs on device.
- Assume a green build means the formatted number is the full value. Host `c++` and ESP-IDF newlib both support `%llu`, so a native check will not catch this.
